</figure>
<figure id="S5.F16" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S5.F16.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.F16.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.F16.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23342v1/combined_io_recall_bar_v3.svg" id="S5.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/125;" width="224" height="125"></object></td>
<td id="S5.F16.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23342v1/combined_cache_hit_rate_bar_v3.svg" id="S5.F16.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/126;" width="224" height="126"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.F16.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.F16.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F16.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(a) Mean I/Os</span></td>
<td id="S5.F16.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F16.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(b) Cache hit ratio</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16. </span>Effect of cache scheme, Recall@10=0.95 on GIST1M</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Effect of cache scheme.</span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
To fully utilize the available memory budget, different cache schemes have been proposed in existing on-disk graph-based solutions.
In particular, DiskANN </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayaram Subramanya et al., 2019</a><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> caches the frequently accessed entry points and their neighbors.
The hybrid cache scheme is devised in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> which combines static cache (with 20% budget) and dynamic cache (with 80% budget). The static cache stores the same content as DiskANN and the dynamic cache employs Least Frequently Used (LFU) policy to dynamically update the cache budget by the visited nodes during query processing.
We compare these existing cache schemes against our proposed in-degree based cache scheme for </span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in this experiment.
Specifically, we replace the degree-based cache scheme of </span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by (i) EP-based cache scheme in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayaram Subramanya et al., 2019</a><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and (ii) hybrid cache scheme in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, respectively.
The tested cache budgets are 0% (0MB), 5% (400MB) and 10% (800MB) of the size of GIST1M.
Figures </span><a href="#S5.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ 5.3. Effectiveness Study ‣ 5. Experimental Evaluation ‣ AlayaLaser: Efficient Index Layout and Search Strategy for Large-scale High-dimensional Vector Similarity Search" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(a) and (b) reported the mean I/Os and cache hit ratio of tested cache schemes.
We can observe: (i) the larger cache budget, the lower mean I/Os and the higher cache hit ratio of all cache schemes;
and (ii) in-degree-based node cache strategy in </span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> consistently outperforms the alternative cache schemes (e.g., EP-based and Hybrid).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Effect of quantization method.</span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
In this experiment, we investigate the effect of different quantization methods on the performance of </span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on GIST1M.
In particular, we test 4 different quantization methods on </span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: (i) PQ with 240 bytes, (ii) PQ with 48 bytes,
(iii) PCA+PQ with 48 bytes, and (iv) PCA+RabitQ with 48 bytes, which is the default setting of </span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Figure </span><a href="#S5.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ 5.3. Effectiveness Study ‣ 5. Experimental Evaluation ‣ AlayaLaser: Efficient Index Layout and Search Strategy for Large-scale High-dimensional Vector Similarity Search" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reports the mean I/Os and throughput of different quantization methods on </span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AlayaLaser</span><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> at different Recall@10.
Firstly, the mean I/Os of PQ (48) is the largest as it severely loses precision due to high compression ratio.
Secondly, PQ (240) has the smallest mean I/Os among all these 4 tested methods but its throughput is obvious below our PCA based methods.
Thirdly, PCA-based methods are better than PQ only method and PCA+RabitQ(48) slightly outperforms PCA+PQ(48), which demonstrates (i) the generality of our PCA-based method and (ii) the efficiency of RabitQ.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F17" class="ltx_figure">
